 ],
 "literal": "Having caressed my wavy dark hair and broad shoulders, / he who departed for the sake of earning wealth, causing my tight bangles to slip off, / will he know? May you live long, my friend! The spotted, striped / fiercely angry snake's hooded head being severed, / as the loud thunder roars in the deep darkness of midnight, / whenever the fine bull moves, there sounds / the sound of a single bell in the stall of many cows.",
 "translation": "My friend, may you live long! He who stroked my wavy dark hair and broad shoulders, and then departed to seek wealth, causing my tight bangles to slip off—does he know? At midnight, in the deep darkness, when the loud thunder roars so fiercely that the hooded heads of angry, striped snakes are severed, whenever the fine bull moves in the stall of many cows, the sound of its single bell rings out.",
 "modern": "தோழியே, நீ வாழ்க! அலை அலையான கரிய கூந்தலையும் பெரிய தோள்களையும் தடவிக் கொடுத்துவிட்டு, இறுக்கமான வளையல்கள் கழன்று வீழுமாறு பொருள் தேடுவதற்காகப் பிரிந்து சென்றவர் அறிவாரோ? புள்ளிகளையும் வரிகளையும் உடைய சினங்கொண்ட பாம்பின் படம் பொருந்திய தலை துண்டிக்கப்பட்டு விழுமாறு, வலிய இடி முழங்குகின்ற நள்ளிரவின் காரிருளில், பல பசுக்கள் கட்டப்பட்டுள்ள தொழுவத்தில் நல்ல காளை அசையும்போதெல்லாம் ஒலிக்கும் அந்த ஒற்றை மணியின் ஓசையை அவர் அறிவாரோ?",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her close friend during the rainy season, which is the promised time of the hero's return. The hero has gone away to earn wealth, leaving her in deep grief, which has caused her to grow thin and her tight bangles to slip off. The poem belongs to the Mullai (forest) landscape, which is associated with patient waiting. The terrifying thunder of the midnight storm, strong enough to sever the heads of angry snakes, contrasts with the gentle, domestic sound of the single bell on the bull in the cow-stall. This contrast highlights the heroine's loneliness and fear in the dark night, as she wonders if her lover, far away, is aware of the painful reminders of his absence.",
